 the  high-ranking officials almost always  lose okay the subtitle of your book  popular support for an undemocratic  regime the changing views of Russians  explain the subtitle well so this book  is the near culmination of about 20 25  years of research with Richard rose a  professor now at Strathclyde University  in Scotland we began really shortly  after the fall of the wall and the  freeing of Eastern Europe concerned with  how ordinary people cope with the  extraordinary events that are associated  with a collapse of a long-standing  regime along with the collapse of the  long-standing economic system in some  cases as in the case of Russia with the  disappearance of the state altogether  same in former Czechoslovakia Yugoslavia  and so on so you had these myriad of  dramatic political changes that affected  citizens and the question was how do  ordinary citizens cope with that and the  early years the question also was to  what extent can citizens learned  citizens who spent their entire lives  under a communist system with a  socialist economy how can they learn to  become democratic citizens and to be  effective  participants in  market economies along the way Russia in  a couple other states turn from what  appeared to be democratic transitions  that to a different form of  authoritarian or semi authoritarianism  and so then the question became what  happens to those citizens who were freed  from authoritarian regimes thought they  might be developing towards a democratic  system and suddenly find themselves at  least slipping backwards into something  quite different from the past but also  quite different from what their  democratic expectations might have been  and we chose to to write that book on on  Russia because it's changes were the  most obvious early on and and it's a  more interesting country to write about  them than Hungary or Romania or one of  the stands from your book  Putin benefited from the popular desire  for the consolidation of order 